+<div id="decoder-f1-followup"><h3 class="reading-heading--topic">F1 dynamics alongside the decoding reference</h3><p>The subsequent F1-only reanalysis compares the same four-odorant panel with DoOR consensus minus receptor-specific spontaneous firing rate. Across tuning receptors, Pearson r / Spearman ρ are +0.317 / +0.406 for 2-heptanone, +0.325 / +0.442 for isopentyl acetate, +0.211 / +0.283 for valeric acid, and −0.002 / +0.006 for pentyl acetate. The comparison includes ten receptors per odorant, except the missing Or42a–valeric acid reference (n = 9). <a href="results.html#f1-dynamic-door">Results presents the two comparison figures</a>.</p><p>These profiles connect the wet-lab observations to the database used for decoding: three odorants share part of its receptor ordering, with a near-zero relationship for pentyl acetate. Reusing the existing reference-pattern backend’s Pearson calculation reproduced all four coefficients.</p></div></section>
 <section class="section-block model-v5" id="decoder-v3-discussion"><h2 class="reading-heading--chapter">Discussion</h2>
 <p>The results support a focused computational conclusion: the UCI representation retains class information through KC activity, while the learned readout loses more. On DoOR, coarse encoding helps a small odorant dataset, but bin count, wiring probability and training duration change together. Controlled ablations are needed to isolate their contributions. The biology motivates the architecture; benchmark scores alone do not show that each circuit motif causes an improvement.</p>
 <p>The UCI split is random rather than day- or batch-held-out. Reusing its held-out set for checkpoints and parameter comparisons makes the best checkpoint exploratory. The DoOR folds are small, and their spread is not independent training-seed replication. For <strong class="media-reference">Figure 7</strong>, the source reports 1,799 rather than the planned 1,800 presentations; it does not document the omitted presentation. Neither benchmark measures food-safety classification or a trained fluorescence-to-odor system.</p>
 <p>The Test 1–DoOR comparison has one well per receptor–odorant condition and does not establish transfer to the cell platform. We propose three to five independent wells per condition, with controls and a prespecified analysis, for the next experiment. Its ±0.043 reference band has no supplied calculation or replicate sampling definition, so it is not a biological uncertainty interval. The source reports 39 pooled pairs but does not supply the complete paired vector or explain the missing fortieth pair; the correlation statistics are retained as reported. This comparison does not use Test 2’s S or VUAA1-normalized N. Likewise, fold-spread lines in the UCI and DoOR plots have no specified SD/SE or confidence-interval definition.</p>
 <p>The source describes an integer implementation, but this update supplies figures and methods rather than matching executable training code, fitted preprocessing, fold assignments, seeds or integer/float agreement tests. We therefore make no new device-speed, energy or integer-accuracy claim. The reader’s current <a href="hardware.html">reference-pattern backend</a> is distinct from a deployed PN–KC–MBON classifier. Earlier KT/UCI analyses, including their clock-error finding, remain in <a href="#decoder-earlier-study">the historical supplement</a>.</p>
-</section>
+<p>The F1 comparison reuses Test 2 recordings under a separately defined, vehicle-referenced metric and the experimenter-confirmed channel map; it is not another independent experiment. Weak across-receptor correlations are evidence of partial profile correspondence, not decoder accuracy. The computational cross-check runs the existing Pearson function only. A PN–KC–MBON inference run would require a fitted input transformation and compatible trained checkpoint, which are not supplied with these fluorescence data.</p></section>
 <section class="section-block model-v5" id="decoder-v3-conclusion"><h2 class="reading-heading--chapter">Conclusion</h2>
 <p>The next computational priority is the KC→MBON readout, evaluated with fixed comparison folds and independent training runs. For receptor data, input resolution must match the number of available odorants.</p>
-</section>
+<p>The F1 profiles provide a measured starting point for comparing the cell platform with the DoOR reference. Their odorant-dependent agreement motivates a future paired evaluation with fixed preprocessing and independently replicated cell measurements.</p></section>
